artifact.self_contained_problem:
In cosmological scenarios where the Peccei–Quinn U(1) symmetry associated with the QCD axion is broken after inflation, a network of global cosmic strings (axion strings) forms and evolves through the radiation-dominated era, ultimately sourcing a population of cold axions that may constitute dark matter. Standard numerical simulations of the resulting "scaling" string network evolve the strings in vacuum and neglect interactions with the surrounding thermal plasma. However, QCD axion strings possess large geometric scattering cross sections with gluons, so the thermal bath of gluons exerts a friction force on the strings.

Develop a quantitative treatment of this thermal friction induced by gluon scattering on axion strings and determine:

(a) how this friction modifies the dynamics of the axion string network (e.g., the approach to and form of the scaling regime, the typical string velocities, the loop production and length distribution, and the spectrum of radiated axions);

(b) whether the inclusion of this friction term substantially changes the predicted number abundance of axions produced by the string network (and consequently the relation between the axion decay constant f_a and the axion dark-matter relic density);

(c) whether the modified dynamics produce any observable signatures (for instance in the axion mass window required for dark matter, in gravitational-wave emission from the network, or in other cosmological observables).

